<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.2 Writing Equations for Point-Slope and Slope-Intercept</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00306</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.2 Writing Equations for Point-Slope and Slope-Intercept</md:title>
    <md:uuid>565c3da7-65cd-453e-8c01-941152cbdfbd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <para id="para-00001"><emphasis>Writing Equations Given Two Points</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00002">For 1 - 3, use the point \((3,4)\) and \((-2, 5)\).</para>
        <exercise id="_69c0e917-231f-4bb7-b26e-c3d1c4e8d7a2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Find the slope of the line that goes through the two points.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Correct! \(-\frac15\) \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\) \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00005">Nice try! The correct answer is \(-\frac15\) \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\) \(m=\frac{5-4}{-2-3}=\frac{1}{-5}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Find the equation in point slope-form of the line that goes through the two points.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\) \(y-4=-\frac{1}{5}(x-3)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Write the equation from question 2 into slope-intercept form.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">(Hint: Solve for \(y\).)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{23}{5}\\y-4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac35\\y-4+4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{3}{5}+4\\y-4+4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{3}{5}+\frac{20}{5}\\y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{1}{5}x+\frac{23}{5}\end{array}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Writing Equations in Slope-Intercept Form Given a Point and Slope</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00014">For 4 - 7 use the point \((3, -2)\) and the slope -4.</para>
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Substitute the slope into slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">\(y= -4x +b\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00018">Substitute the given point in for \((x, y)\) in the equation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">\(-2= -4(3) +b\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <exercise id="ee65d7ce-dddd-4fce-ba53-ad14f6eff791" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00021">Solve for \(b\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00022">Correct! 10</para>
            <para id="para-00023">Nice try! The correct answer is 10.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00024">Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">\(y= -4x +10\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00027">With a partner, work to go directly from two points to an equation in slope-intercept form, without using point-slope form.</para>
      <para id="para-00028">For 8 - 12, use the two points \((5, 2)\) and \((3, -1)\)</para>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <exercise id="_86409435-3d6d-4faa-ae2e-7ecb87fbf9a3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00029">Find the slope between the two points.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00030">Correct! \(\frac32\) \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\) \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac32\) \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\) \(m=\frac{-1-2}{3-5}=\frac{-3}{-2}=\frac32\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00032">Substitute the slope into slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00034">\(y= \frac{3}{2}x +b\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00035">Substitute the one point in for \((x, y)\) in the equation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037">\(5= \frac{3}{2}(2) +b\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <exercise id="_8ae75025-f392-46a3-b8d9-27ec322c8f70" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00038">Solve for \(b\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00039">Correct! 2</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Nice try! The correct answer is 2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#12-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00041">Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00042">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00043">\(y= \frac{3}{2}x +2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00044">Which of the following is the equation of a line that has a slope of 3 and goes through the point \((1, -2)\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Find an Equation Given Two Points</title>
      <section id="sect-00007">
        <title>Method 1: Using Point-Slope Form</title>
        <para id="para-00045">Find an equation of a line that contains the points \((5, 4)\) and \((3, 6)\). Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope between the two points. \(m=\frac{6-4}{3-5}=\frac{2}{-2}=-1\)</para>
        <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Choose a point to substitute into point-slope form. \(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\) \(y-4=-1(x-5)\) OR \(y-6=-1(x-3) \)</para>
        <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00049">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-4&amp;=&amp;-1(x-5)\\y-4&amp;=&amp;-1x+5\\y&amp;=&amp;-x +9\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00050">OR</para>
        <para id="para-00051">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-6&amp;=&amp;-1(x-3)\\ y-6&amp;=&amp;-1x+3\\y&amp;=&amp;-x +9\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Method 2: Writing an Equation in Slope-Intercept Form by finding \(b\).</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00053">Find the equation of the line containing the points \((3, 1)\) and \((5, 6)\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope between the two points. \(m=\frac{6-1}{5-3}=\frac{5}{2}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Choose a point to substitute into slope-intercept form then solve for \(b\).  \(y=mx+b\)</para>
        <para id="para-00056">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;\frac{5}{2}x+b\\1&amp;=&amp;\frac{5}{2}(3)+b\\\frac{2}{2}&amp;=&amp;\frac{15}{2}+b\\b&amp;=&amp;\frac{13}{2}\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00057">OR</para>
        <para id="para-00058">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;\frac{5}{2}x+b\\6&amp;=&amp;\frac{5}{2}(5)+b\\\frac{12}{2}&amp;=&amp;\frac{25}{2}+b\\b&amp;=&amp;\frac{13}{2}\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form. \(y=\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{13}{2}\)</para>
      </section>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Find an Equation Given Two Points</title>
        <!--Q#-->
          <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00060">Find the equation of a line that consists of the points \((1, 4)\) and \((6, 2)\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
            <para id="para-00061">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00062">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00063">\(y=-\frac{2}{5}x+\frac{22}{5}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00064"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope between the two points. \(m=\frac{2-4}{6-1}=\frac{-2}{5}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00065"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Choose a point to substitute into slope-intercept form then solve for \(b\).  \(y=mx+b\)</para>
            <para id="para-00066">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{2}{5}x+b\\4&amp;=&amp;-\frac{2}{5}(1)+b\\\frac{20}{5}&amp;=&amp;-\frac{2}{5}+b\\b&amp;=&amp;\frac{22}{5}\end{array}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00067">OR</para>
            <para id="para-00068">\(\begin{array}{rcl}y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{2}{5}x+b\\2&amp;=&amp;-\frac{2}{5}(6)+b\\\frac{10}{5}&amp;=&amp;-\frac{12}{5}+b\\b&amp;=&amp;\frac{22}{5}\end{array}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the equation in slope-intercept form. \(y=-\frac{2}{5}x+\frac{22}{5}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
